  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The Parc naturel départemental du Vinaigrier sits on the heights above Nice, between the Riquier district and the Col de Villefranche, following an old water-supply channel converted into a walking trail. The route climbs gently to around 370 metres of altitude, offering views over the city, the surrounding hills and the sea, in a setting of pines and scrubland that feels much quieter than the seafront.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The trail, flat for a good part of its length (a former channel of the Compagnie Générale des Eaux), remains easy and suitable for the whole family, covering about 2 to 3 km in roughly an hour. As in all of the Alpes-Maritimes' departmental nature parks, the rules require dogs to wear a collar and stay on a leash year-round; category-1 dogs are banned and category-2 dogs must be muzzled. Access is via the Chemin du Vinaigrier, followed by a few steps up to the start of the trail.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: a good option for a short, shaded walk with your dog without leaving Nice, especially in the late afternoon once the heat eases off.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Mont Boron and its park, reachable on foot or by car, let you extend the outing with another view over the Baie des Anges and Nice's harbour.</p>
